"[2604.19753] Algorithm Selection with Zero Domain Knowledge via Text Embeddings (https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19753)\nciteturn32137view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19753\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 20 Mar 2026 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 5 Oct 2026 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:Algorithm Selection with Zero Domain Knowledge via Text Embeddings\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Stefan Szeider L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Algorithm Selection with Zero Domain Knowledge via Text Embeddings, by Stefan Szeider\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We propose ZeroFolio, a feature-free approach to algorithm selection that uses pretrained text embeddings instead of hand-crafted instance features. It reads the raw instance file as plain text, embeds it with a pretrained embedding model, and selects an algorithm via weighted k-nearest neighbors. Our approach is based on the observation that pretrained embeddings can distinguish problem instances without any domain knowledge or task-specific training.\nL17: ZeroFolio applies to any problem domain with text-based instance formats. We evaluate our approach on 11 ASlib scenarios spanning 7 domains (SAT, MaxSAT, QBF, ASP, CSP, MIP, and graph problems). ZeroFolio outperforms a random forest trained on hand-crafted features in 9 of 11 scenarios, often substantially, and in 8 of them with every serialization seed. It wins 8 of 11 scenarios against a per-scenario-tuned random forest.\nL18: On the three scenarios with published AutoFolio results from the 2015 ICON Challenge, ZeroFolio comes within a small margin of AutoFolio without any per-scenario tuning. Our ablation study on SAT12-ALL shows that inverse-distance weighting and line shuffling improve performance. We further analyze the sensitivity of our approach to the serialization seed. On the SAT12-ALL scenario, where the random forest is stronger, both methods can be combined via soft voting to achieve further improvements.\nL19: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2604.19753 [cs.AI]\nL21:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2604.19753v3 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL22:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.19753†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Stefan Szeider [cite12†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Fri, 20 Mar 2026 13:07:59 UTC (299 KB)\nL27: cite13†[v2] Sat, 11 Jul 2026 09:49:29 UTC (307 KB)\nL28: [v3] Mon, 5 Oct 2026 19:26:23 UTC (307 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Algorithm Selection with Zero Domain Knowledge via Text Embeddings, by Stefan Szeider\nL35: \nL36:   * cite7†View PDF L37:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite14†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite15†view license L41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * cite16†Audio Summary L45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.AI\nL48: \nL49: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L50: \nL51: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2026-04 L52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: cite22†cs L56: cite23†cs.CL L57: cite24†cs.LG L58: \nL59: ### References & Citations\nL60: \nL61:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L62:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L63:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L64: \nL65: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL66: \nL67: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL68: \nL69: [Bu